<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konstantinos Karamanlis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Constantine Karamanlis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Konstantinos Karamanlis was a Greek conservative statesman, prime minister from 1955 to 1963 and from 1974 to 1980, and president from 1980 to 1985 and 1990 to 1995. Returning from exile when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> collapsed in 1974, he led the transition to democracy known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metapolitefsi">Metapolitefsi</a>, founded the New Democracy party and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Community</a> in 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konstantinos-karamanlis.jpg" alt="Konstantinos Karamanlis" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bert Verhoeff for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bezoek_premier_Karamanlis_van_Griekenland_Van_Agt_in_gesprek_met_Karamanlis,_Bestanddeelnr_929-6548_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Konstantinos Karamanlis was born on 8 March 1907 in the village of Proti, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He became a Greek citizen in 1913, when Macedonia was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> after the First and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a>. He studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, practised in Serres, and in 1936, at the age of 28, was first elected to parliament for the conservative People's Party. After the war he rose quickly, becoming Minister of Labour in 1947 and, in 1952, Minister of Public Works under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandros-papagos">Alexandros Papagos</a>, when the Greek Rally won the election.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Papagos died in October 1955, King Paul appointed the 48-year-old Karamanlis prime minister, bypassing two senior party figures. He extended full voting rights to women, and won elections in 1956, 1958 and 1961. He also gave up enosis, union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, in favour of independence for the island, through the Zurich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> agreements. On 9 July 1961 his government signed an association treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>.</p><p>The assassination of the leftist deputy Grigoris Lambrakis in May 1963 weakened his position, and that summer he resigned after a clash with King Paul over a royal visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He then spent eleven years in self-imposed exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, while a military coup took place on 21 April 1967.</p><p>After the junta collapsed in July 1974 he was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to lead a national unity government. He legalised the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, freed political prisoners and held elections in November 1974, in which his new party, New Democracy, won 54.4 percent. A plebiscite then abolished the monarchy, and the junta's leaders were tried. A Turkish invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> on 14 August 1974 led him to withdraw Greece from the military side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> until 1980.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Karamanlis signed the treaty of accession to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in May 1979, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> became its tenth member on 1 January 1981, while he was president, a post he held from 1980 to 1985 and again from 1990 to 1995. He won the Karlspreis in 1978. He died in 1998 at 91 and was buried on the grounds of the foundation he had founded.</p><p>Assessments differ. Supporters called him the Ethnarches, or national leader, and he is credited with restoring democracy and with Greek entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Left-wing opponents accused him of condoning right-wing para-state groups. He has also been criticised for his handling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1959 and 1974.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Konstantinos Karamanlis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Konstantinos Karamanlis</b>, the dominant politician of the post-war years, was invited back from self-exile, and the first multiparty elections since 1964 were held on the first anniversary of the Polytechnic uprising.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1946 the restored monarchy ruled through conservative governments under Alexandros Papagos and <b>Konstantinos Karamanlis</b>, with left-wing parties restricted, until the crisis of 1965 between King Constantine II and George Papandreou.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Konstantinos Karamanlis</b> returned from exile the next day to restore democracy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-23">23 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Proti, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Hygeia Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.94,24&amp;year=1966" aria-label="Proti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 702 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Konstantinos Karamanlis"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5587 972h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5587 972h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.94,24&amp;year=1966">What was here in 1966? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
